\paragraph{Code ($\Kempirical{=}2$): cross-modality confirmation.}
Python functions from CodeSearchNet~\cite{husain2019codesearchnet} ($n{=}5{,}000$, Qwen2.5-1.5B) yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ (1v2 $\auc = 0.738$, CI $[0.709, 0.767]$; 2v3 $\auc = 0.530$; Table~\ref{tab:code_domain_qwen} in Appendix).
Despite a different modality, code matches C4 web text ($\Kempirical{=}2$), with higher 1v2 AUC (0.74 vs.\ 0.61--0.70) but the same sharp 2v3 collapse (JSD ratio 1v2/2v3 $= 11.7\times$), indicating that syntactic constraints provide stronger per-step signal without extending the boundary.

\paragraph{Domain as the primary factor.}
With the scorer confound controlled, the domain ordering is uniform across all tested models: arXiv ($\Kempirical{=}3$) $>$ C4/Code ($\Kempirical{=}2$) $>$ Wikipedia ($\Kempirical{=}1$).
Neither model scale (1B--14B), architecture family, nor modality (natural language vs.\ code) alters this ordering; source-domain structural complexity is the primary determinant of $\Kempirical$.

